# Beamline Assistant Configuration Template
# Copy this file to .env and customize for your installation

# =============================================================================
# ANL AUTHENTICATION (REQUIRED)
# =============================================================================
# Your Argonne National Laboratory username for Argo API access
ANL_USERNAME=your_anl_username

# =============================================================================
# AI MODEL SELECTION (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================
# Default AI model for analysis
# Options: gpt4o, gpt4turbo, gpt4, claudesonnet4, claudeopus4, gemini25pro, gemini25flash
ARGO_MODEL=gpt4o

# =============================================================================
# MIDAS INSTALLATION PATH (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================
# Path to your MIDAS installation
# If not set, the system will automatically search for MIDAS in:
#   1. ~/.MIDAS (beamline standard)
#   2. ~/MIDAS
#   3. ~/opt/MIDAS (macOS/development)
#   4. /opt/MIDAS (system-wide Linux)
#   5. ./MIDAS (current directory)

# Common beamline configuration (Linux):
# MIDAS_PATH=~/.MIDAS

# Development configuration (macOS):
# MIDAS_PATH=~/opt/MIDAS

# System-wide configuration:
# MIDAS_PATH=/opt/MIDAS

# Custom path:
# MIDAS_PATH=/path/to/your/MIDAS

# Leave commented out for automatic detection:
# MIDAS_PATH=

# =============================================================================
# REMOTE EXECUTION / TOPOLOGY (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================
# APEXA often runs on a different host than the one holding the synchrotron data
# and MIDAS compute (e.g. APEXA on `chiltepin`, data + MIDAS on `copland:/gdata`).
# Data-dependent MIDAS tools (calibrate_ring_thresholds, run_ff_hedm_full_workflow)
# route their execution over SSH to the host that owns the input data, so the tool
# runs where the data lives. The local path is unchanged: if the data is local, the
# tool runs locally exactly as before. Requires KEY-BASED SSH to the remote host(s).
#
# --- Multi-host registry (preferred for >1 remote host / >1 technique) ----------
# Copy remote_hosts.example.json -> remote_hosts.json (gitignored) and edit it.
# It maps a data-root prefix to the analysis host that owns it (longest prefix wins),
# with a per-host `activate` snippet (conda/venv) and optional `midas_path`.
# APEXA_REMOTE_HOSTS_FILE=/path/to/remote_hosts.json   # default: repo sibling of servers.config
#
# --- Single-host fallback (no registry file needed) -----------------------------
# If remote_hosts.json is absent, one remote host is synthesized from these:
# APEXA_ANALYSIS_HOST=copland                 # default remote host (also used by run_remote_command)
# APEXA_REMOTE_DATA_ROOTS=/gdata              # ':'-separated prefixes owned by that host
# APEXA_REMOTE_MIDAS_ACTIVATE=conda deactivate && export PATH=/home/beams12/S1IDUSER/opt/envs/midas/bin:$PATH
#   ^ the MIDAS developer's guidance (2026-09-14): the pip midas-suite does
#     everything and needs no repo clone. `conda deactivate` is required --
#     an active midas_env shadows that bin even after PATH is set.
# APEXA_REMOTE_MIDAS_PATH=/home/beams/.../MIDAS          # git-tree fallback if console scripts absent
#
# --- LOCAL MIDAS runtime: use the pip midas-suite, not the C++ tree ----------
# APEXA_MIDAS_BIN is the maintained `midas-suite` env's bin directory. Set it and
# APEXA (a) looks there FIRST when resolving every midas-* console script, and
# (b) deactivates conda for the MIDAS subprocess -- dropping conda bins from PATH
# and clearing CONDA_PREFIX/PYTHONHOME, which a child python honours ahead of
# PATH. Unset => the previous C++-first behaviour, unchanged.
#
# At APS the shared env is on /home/beams* and is visible from every beamline host:
# APEXA_MIDAS_BIN=/home/beams12/S1IDUSER/opt/envs/midas/bin
#
# This does NOT configure your interactive shell -- it only affects processes
# APEXA launches. For your own `midas-*` / floorcheck.py / py-spy commands still run:
#     conda deactivate
#     export PATH=/home/beams12/S1IDUSER/opt/envs/midas/bin:$PATH
#
# APEXA_MIDAS_DEVICE=auto     # auto | cpu | cuda -- torch device for midas-calibrate-v2.
#                             # auto picks cuda when available. Was hardcoded cpu,
#                             # which made a differentiable fit take minutes/frame.
# APEXA_MIDAS_THREADS=8       # bound torch CPU threads so back-to-back or parallel
#                             # single-frame calibrations stop fighting each other.

# --- Force override (debugging) -------------------------------------------------
# APEXA_FORCE_REMOTE_EXEC=1   # force remote on the resolved/default host (bypass locality inference)
# APEXA_FORCE_REMOTE_EXEC=0   # force local even for a registered remote data root
# (Any tool also accepts an explicit host="..." argument that overrides all inference.)

# =============================================================================
# NETWORK TIER (OPTIONAL) — run APEXA on a host with no public internet
# =============================================================================
# APEXA can run on a beamline host that has data + the ANL-internal network but
# NO route to the public internet (e.g. `copland`). The tier controls what the
# runtime is allowed to reach, and — crucially — is set so a no-internet host
# never *blocks* trying to reach the web (a network hang is not an exception, so
# it cannot be caught; the tier prevents the attempt).
#
#   APEXA_NETWORK=internal   # DEFAULT. ANL-internal only: Argo/argo-proxy, ALCF,
#                            #   SSH to analysis hosts. All data reduction works.
#                            #   RAG loads from the pre-staged embedder cache and
#                            #   never touches the network. HuggingFace is forced
#                            #   offline; ChromaDB telemetry is disabled.
#   APEXA_NETWORK=web        # Also the public internet: first-time HuggingFace
#                            #   model download, Materials Project, DOI metadata.
#   APEXA_NETWORK=data       # Filesystem only (no LLM) — validation/tests.
#
# `docs/setup_user.sh` probes reachability and writes this for you.
#
# Offline install: `uv sync` (no extras) has NO public-internet dependency — the
# RAG stack (chromadb + sentence-transformers) stays in the base install and runs
# offline from a staged cache (see docs/OFFLINE_DEPLOYMENT.md for pre-staging the
# ~500 MB Nomic embedder). The ONLY online-only feature is Materials Project CIF
# fetch (`fetch_cif_from_mp`), whose `mp-api` package lives in a separate extra:
#   uv sync --extra mp     # ONLY on a web-capable host (APEXA_NETWORK=web)
# Omit it on internal/offline hosts — the tool is hidden below the `web` tier and,
# if reached, returns a structured "unavailable at this tier" error (never hangs).
# APEXA_NETWORK=internal
#
# APEXA_KB_PREWARM=1   # allow the RAG embedder pre-warm below `web` tier — set
#                      #   ONLY once ~/.cache/huggingface is genuinely staged,
#                      #   else startup blocks on the first-run download.

# --- Beamline / technique scope gate --------------------------------------
# APEXA_BEAMLINE — the APS sector this host serves (e.g. 1-ID-E, 8-ID-E, 6-ID-C,
# 17-BM, 20-D-E). When set, the scope gate at the tool chokepoint refuses a MIDAS
# tool whose technique capsule is scoped to an *incompatible* beamline (segment-
# prefix rule: 1-ID-E is compatible with 1-ID; 6-ID-C conflicts with 1-ID-E), so
# the agent can't drive, say, a 1-ID-only workflow on a 6-ID host. Leave UNSET to
# disable the gate entirely (never blocks — the safe default for a general host).
# A technique whose capsule names no beamline always fails open (no block).
# APEXA_BEAMLINE=1-ID-E
#
# APEXA_IGNORE_SCOPE_GATE=1   # escape hatch — run the tool even when the scope
#                             #   gate would refuse it (e.g. a cross-sector test).

# =============================================================================
# ADDITIONAL ANALYSIS TOOLS (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================
# Paths for additional analysis software
# These are auto-detected if not set, similar to MIDAS
# Enable/disable servers in servers.config

# GSAS-II - Rietveld refinement and powder diffraction
# Auto-detects: ~/GSASII, ~/.local/GSASII, /opt/GSASII
# GSAS2_PATH=~/GSASII

# MAUD - Independent Rietveld engine, accessed through `run_gsas_refinement(engine="maud")`
# via the MILK Python wrapper (lanl/MILK). Optional: absence is non-fatal — engine="maud"
# returns a structured engine_unavailable response, engine="both" silently falls back to
# gsas2-only with fallback_used="gsas2".
# Auto-detects: $MAUD_PATH, ~/MAUD, ~/.local/MAUD, /opt/MAUD,
#               /Applications/Maud.app/Contents/Resources (macOS)
# Install MILK: `uv pip install milk-rietveld` (requires a working JDK on PATH)
# MAUD_PATH=~/MAUD
# JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/default-java   # only set if your JDK is in a non-standard location

# PyFAI - Fast azimuthal integration
# Usually installed via pip: uv add pyFAI
# PYFAI_PATH=/path/to/pyfai

# DIOPTAS - Interactive data processing
# DIOPTAS_PATH=~/DIOPTAS

# =============================================================================
# DEPLOYMENT NOTES
# =============================================================================
#
# For Beamline Deployment:
# 1. Each user should have their own .env file with their ANL_USERNAME
# 2. Install analysis tools at standard locations (~/.MIDAS, ~/GSASII, etc.)
# 3. Enable only needed servers in servers.config
# 4. Ensure all users have access to the Argo Gateway (inside ANL network)
# 5. Use uv for package management: uv add <package>
#
# For Development:
# 1. Set tool paths (MIDAS_PATH, GSAS2_PATH, etc.) to your local installations
# 2. Use your personal ANL_USERNAME
# 3. Test with different models (gpt4o recommended for speed)
# 4. Install dependencies with: uv add <package>
#
# For Adding New Tools:
# 1. Create server in servers/ directory (see ADDING_NEW_SERVERS.md)
# 2. Add to servers.config: toolname:servers/toolname_server.py
# 3. Optionally add path configuration here
# 4. Install dependencies: uv add required-packages
#
# Security:
# - Never commit .env files to version control
# - Each user's credentials stay private
# - .env is listed in .gitignore
